@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using RonSijm.Blazyload.Components
@using RonSijm.Demo.Blazyload.Components.Pizza.Contracts
@attribute [BlazyComponent("burger.editor")]

<h1>Burger editor</h1>
<p>Simple demo: compose components by name, with minimal changes and dependencies. No source generator or generated contracts are needed.</p>
<p>Burger asks for a named component, not a Pizza implementation type:</p>
<pre class="simple-code" data-testid="simple-consumer-code"><code>@ConsumerCode</code></pre>
<p>
    Open your browser's developer tools (F12), select the <strong>Network</strong> tab, and filter for <code>Pizza</code> before clicking the button below.
    Watch the Pizza assemblies (DLLs) and service dependency load on demand when you click <strong>Show pizza calendar</strong>.
    This .NET 10 sample serves assemblies as <code>.wasm</code> files, sometimes with fingerprinted filenames.
</p>
<button data-testid="show-pizza" @onclick="ShowPizza">Show pizza calendar</button>
<button data-testid="change-title" @onclick="ChangeTitle">Change calendar title</button>
<p data-testid="selected-date">@_selectedDate</p>

@if (_showPizza)
{
    <BlazyComponent Name="pizza.calendar" Parameters="@_parameters" />
}

<section>
    <h2>Pass ordinary Blazor parameters and a callback</h2>
    <p>The dictionary keys match the calendar's parameter properties. The model comes from a tiny handwritten contracts project; the callback reports the chosen date to Burger.</p>
    <pre class="simple-code" data-testid="simple-parameters-code"><code>@ParametersCode</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Two startup calls, then publish the producer lazily</h2>
    <p>Configure the WebAssembly host before <code>builder.Build()</code>. The host references Pizza for deployment; Burger references only Components and the handwritten model.</p>
    <pre class="simple-code" data-testid="simple-startup-code"><code>@StartupCode</code></pre>
    <p>In the host project file, mark Pizza's implementation as lazy. The component catalog is generated during the build.</p>
    <pre class="simple-code" data-testid="simple-publication-code"><code>@PublicationCode</code></pre>
</section>

@code {
    private const string ConsumerCode = "<BlazyComponent Name=\"pizza.calendar\" Parameters=\"@_parameters\" />";
    private const string ParametersCode = """
        _parameters = new Dictionary<string, object?>
        {
            ["Title"] = "Pizza for burgers",
            ["Model"] = new PizzaCalendarModel("Burger customer"),
            ["DateSelected"] = EventCallback.Factory.Create<DateOnly>(this, HandleDateSelected)
        };

        private void HandleDateSelected(DateOnly date)
        {
            _selectedDate = date.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd");
        }
        """;
    private const string StartupCode = """
        builder.UseBlazyload();
        builder.Services.AddBlazyloadComponents();
        """;
    private const string PublicationCode = """
        <ItemGroup>
          <BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad Include="RonSijm.Demo.Blazyload.Components.Pizza.wasm" />
        </ItemGroup>
        """;
    private bool _showPizza;
    private string? _selectedDate;
    private Dictionary<string, object?> _parameters = [];

    private void ShowPizza()
    {
        _parameters = new Dictionary<string, object?>
        {
            ["Title"] = "Pizza for burgers",
            ["Model"] = new PizzaCalendarModel("Burger customer"),
            ["DateSelected"] = EventCallback.Factory.Create<DateOnly>(this, HandleDateSelected)
        };
        _showPizza = true;
    }

    private void ChangeTitle()
    {
        _parameters = new Dictionary<string, object?>(_parameters)
        {
            ["Title"] = "Updated pizza calendar"
        };
    }

    private void HandleDateSelected(DateOnly date)
    {
        _selectedDate = date.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd");
    }
}
